FSTAB(5)                    File Formats                     FSTAB(5)

NAME
    fstab - filesystems mounted at boot

SYNOPSIS
    /etc/fstab

DESCRIPTION
    Each line names a device, a mount point, a filesystem type and an
    optional comma separated list of options; # starts a comment. The
    device is a name under /dev, with or without the /dev/ prefix, or
    PARTUUID=GUID for the partition with that unique GUID, as
    /dev/partitions lists it. The types are mfs, fat and tmpfs, a
    filesystem in memory whose device field is ignored.

OPTIONS
    defaults   no option
    nofail     a device that cannot be mounted is skipped without error
    noauto     the entry is not mounted by fsinit
    seed=DIR   copy the tree below DIR into the mount point when the
               mounted filesystem is empty
    homes      the volume holds the home directories: convert it from the
               single user layout once and make missing homes (fsinit(1))
    uid=N      owner of every file of a fat volume (default 0)
    gid=N      group of every file of a fat volume (default 0)
    umask=OOO  permission bits removed from the files of a fat volume
               (default 022), which keeps no permissions of its own
    size=MIB   the largest size of a tmpfs (default a quarter of the
               memory)
    mode=OOOO  permission bits of the root directory of a tmpfs
               (default 1777)

    The options from uid on are passed to the filesystem, tmpfs takes
    uid and gid as the owner of its root directory, and mfs accepts
    none, since it stores owners and permissions itself.

EXAMPLE
    vdc /home mfs nofail,seed=/usr/share/skel/home,homes
    PARTUUID=c0ffee00-0000-4000-8000-000000000001 /boot fat
    tmpfs /tmp tmpfs mode=1777

SEE ALSO
    fsinit(1), mount(1)
